NNAAMMEE
       more - file perusal filter for crt viewing

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       mmoorree [options] _f_i_l_e ...

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       mmoorree is a filter for paging through text one screenful at a time. This
       version is especially primitive. Users should realize that lleessss(1)
       provides mmoorree(1) emulation plus extensive enhancements.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       Options are also taken from the environment variable MMOORREE (make sure to
       precede them with a dash (--)) but command-line options will override
       those.

       --dd, ----ssiilleenntt
           Prompt with "[Press space to continue, 'q' to quit.]", and display
           "[Press 'h' for instructions.]" instead of ringing the bell when an
           illegal key is pressed.

       --ll, ----llooggiiccaall
           Do not pause after any line containing a ^^LL (form feed).

       --ee, ----eexxiitt--oonn--eeooff
           Exit on End-Of-File, enabled by default if not executed on
           terminal.

       --ff, ----nnoo--ppaauussee
           Count logical lines, rather than screen lines (i.e., long lines are
           not folded).

       --pp, ----pprriinntt--oovveerr
           Do not scroll. Instead, clear the whole screen and then display the
           text. Notice that this option is switched on automatically if the
           executable is named ppaaggee.

       --cc, ----cclleeaann--pprriinntt
           Do not scroll. Instead, paint each screen from the top, clearing
           the remainder of each line as it is displayed.

       --ss, ----ssqquueeeezzee
           Squeeze multiple blank lines into one.

       --uu, ----ppllaaiinn
           Suppress underlining. This option is silently ignored as backwards
           compatibility.

       --nn, ----lliinneess _n_u_m_b_e_r
           Specify the _n_u_m_b_e_r of lines per screenful. The _n_u_m_b_e_r argument is a
           positive decimal integer. The ----lliinneess option shall override any
           values obtained from any other source, such as number of lines
           reported by terminal.

       --_n_u_m_b_e_r
           A numeric option means the same as ----lliinneess option argument.

       ++_n_u_m_b_e_r
           Start displaying each file at line _n_u_m_b_e_r.

       ++/_s_t_r_i_n_g
           The _s_t_r_i_n_g to be searched in each file before starting to display
           it.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
       Interactive commands for mmoorree are based on vvii(1). Some commands may be
       preceded by a decimal number, called k in the descriptions below. In
       the following descriptions, ^^XX means ccoonnttrrooll--XX.

       hh or ??
           Help; display a summary of these commands. If you forget all other
           commands, remember this one.

       SSPPAACCEE
           Display next k lines of text. Defaults to current screen size.

       zz
           Display next k lines of text. Defaults to current screen size.
           Argument becomes new default.

       RREETTUURRNN
           Display next k lines of text. Defaults to 1. Argument becomes new
           default.

       dd or ^^DD
           Scroll k lines. Default is current scroll size, initially 11.
           Argument becomes new default.

       qq or QQ or IINNTTEERRRRUUPPTT
           Exit.

       ss
           Skip forward k lines of text. Defaults to 1.

       ff
           Skip forward k screenfuls of text. Defaults to 1.

       bb or ^^BB
           Skip backwards k screenfuls of text. Defaults to 1. Only works with
           files, not pipes.

       ''
           Go to the place where the last search started.

       ==
           Display current line number.

       //ppaatttteerrnn
           Search for kth occurrence of regular expression. Defaults to 1.

       nn
           Search for kth occurrence of last regular expression. Defaults to
           1.

       !!ccoommmmaanndd or ::!!ccoommmmaanndd
           Execute _c_o_m_m_a_n_d in a subshell.

       vv
           Start up an editor at current line. The editor is taken from the
           environment variable VVIISSUUAALL if defined, or EEDDIITTOORR if VVIISSUUAALL is not
           defined, or defaults to vvii(1) if neither VVIISSUUAALL nor EEDDIITTOORR is
           defined.

       ^^LL
           Redraw screen.

       ::nn
           Go to kth next file. Defaults to 1.

       ::pp
           Go to kth previous file. Defaults to 1.

       ::ff
           Display current file name and line number.

       ..
           Repeat previous command.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
       The mmoorree command respects the following environment variables, if they
       exist:

       MMOORREE
           This variable may be set with favored options to mmoorree.

       SSHHEELLLL
           Current shell in use (normally set by the shell at login time).

       TTEERRMM
           The terminal type used by mmoorree to get the terminal characteristics
           necessary to manipulate the screen.

       VVIISSUUAALL
           The editor the user prefers. Invoked when command key _v is pressed.

       EEDDIITTOORR
           The editor of choice when VVIISSUUAALL is not specified.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       The mmoorree command appeared in 3.0BSD. This man page documents mmoorree
       version 5.19 (Berkeley 6/29/88), which is currently in use in the Linux
       community. Documentation was produced using several other versions of
       the man page, and extensive inspection of the source code.

